## Claim
Text: "Documents show the CIA made a referral to the FBI documenting the allegation that U.S. Presidential candidate Hillary Clinton had approved a campaign plan to stir up a scandal against U.S. Presidential candidate Donald Trump by tying him to Putin and the Russians’ hacking of the Democratic National Committee."
Claim date: October 07, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### 1. Is there documentary evidence, such as an official memo or report, showing that the Central Intelligence Agency (CIA) made a formal referral to the Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) in 2016?
Answer: Yes, there is documentary evidence. The source explicitly states that "a referral sent from the CIA to FBI Director James Comey in 2016" was declassified and released in "partly redacted, three-page referral (pdf)" form to members of Congress.

Source URL: https://amac.us/newsline/society/dni-declassifies-cia-memo-that-comey-claimed-to-have-no-memory-of-receiving/

### 2. If a CIA referral to the FBI exists, does it explicitly document an allegation that Hillary Clinton approved a campaign plan?
Answer: Yes, the CIA referral explicitly documents that Hillary Clinton approved a campaign plan. The CIA memo states it references "an exchange discussing U.S. presidential candidate Hillary Clinton’s approval of a plan concerning U.S. presidential candidate Donald Trump and Russian hackers."

Source URL: https://news.yahoo.com/dni-releases-cia-documents-hillary-204337457.html

### 3. Does the alleged plan described in the documentation specify that its goal was to "stir up a scandal" against Donald Trump?
Answer: Yes, the alleged plan explicitly specified "to stir up a scandal" as its goal, as directly stated in the declassified document: "...Hillary Clinton had approved a campaign plan to stir up a scandal against U.S. Presidential candidate Donald Trump..." and again in Director Brennan's briefing notes describing a proposal "to vilify Donald Trump by stirring up a scandal."

Source URL: https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/09-29-20_Letter%20to%20Sen.%20Graham_Declassification%20of%20FBI's%20Crossfire%20Hurricane%20Investigations_20-00912_U_SIGNED-FINAL.pdf

### 4. Does the documentation of the alleged plan describe its objective as linking Donald Trump to Russian President Vladimir Putin?
Answer: Yes, the documentation explicitly describes the objective as "tying him to Putin," as stated in the declassified intelligence analysis: "Hillary Clinton had approved a campaign plan to stir up a scandal against U.S. Presidential candidate Donald Trump by tying him to Putin and the Russians’ hacking of the Democratic National Committee."

Source URL: https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/09-29-20_Letter%20to%20Sen.%20Graham_Declassification%20of%20FBI's%20Crossfire%20Hurricane%20Investigations_20-00912_U_SIGNED-FINAL.pdf

### 5. Does the documentation of the alleged plan describe its objective as linking Donald Trump to the Russian government's hacking of the Democratic National Committee (DNC)?
Answer: No, the documentation does not specifically describe the objective as linking Donald Trump to the Russian government's hacking of the DNC. The declassified CIA memo refers to "a plan concerning U.S. presidential candidate Donald Trump and Russian hackers hampering U.S. elections," but it does not explicitly mention the Democratic National Committee hack, focusing instead on broader election interference as a distraction.

Source URL: https://news.yahoo.com/dni-releases-cia-documents-hillary-204337457.html

### 6. What is the original source of the allegation presented in the CIA referral (e.g., a human intelligence source, a foreign intelligence service, open-source reporting)?
Answer: The original source of the allegation presented in the CIA referral was "a Russian intelligence analysis obtained by U.S. authorities," as stated by Director of National Intelligence John Ratcliffe.

Source URL: https://amac.us/newsline/society/dni-declassifies-cia-memo-that-comey-claimed-to-have-no-memory-of-receiving/

### 7. Does the documentation indicate that the alleged plan was presented as a verified fact or as an unverified intelligence allegation?
Answer: The documentation explicitly presents the alleged plan as an **unverified intelligence allegation**, stating that "The IC does not know the accuracy of this allegation or the extent to which the Russian intelligence analysis may reflect exaggeration or fabrication."

Source URL: https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/09-29-20_Letter%20to%20Sen.%20Graham_Declassification%20of%20FBI's%20Crossfire%20Hurricane%20Investigations_20-00912_U_SIGNED-FINAL.pdf

### 8. When is the alleged plan by Hillary Clinton purported to have been approved according to the documentation?
Answer: According to the declassified summary cited in the source, Hillary Clinton allegedly approved the plan on July 26, 2016.

Source URL: https://amac.us/newsline/society/dni-declassifies-cia-memo-that-comey-claimed-to-have-no-memory-of-receiving/

### 9. Is there any documentation from the FBI indicating it received and acted upon the specific referral from the CIA described in the claim?
Answer: Yes, there is documentation indicating the FBI received the referral: a declassified CIA memo was sent directly to then-FBI Director James Comey and Peter Strzok, stating it was provided "for the exclusive use of your bureau for background investigative action or lead purposes as appropriate." However, Comey testified he did not remember receiving it, creating ambiguity about any subsequent action taken.

Source URL: https://news.yahoo.com/dni-releases-cia-documents-hillary-204337457.html

### 10. What is the provenance and authenticity status of the "documents" cited in the original claim (e.g., declassified government documents, leaked materials, journalistic reporting)?
Answer: The "documents" cited in the claim are authentic, declassified U.S. government materials officially released by the Director of National Intelligence (DNI), specifically including former CIA Director John Brennan's handwritten notes and a CIA referral memo.

Source URL: https://news.yahoo.com/dni-releases-cia-documents-hillary-204337457.html

## Final Judgement
The fact-check confirms that a declassified CIA referral to the FBI in 2016 documented an intelligence allegation that Hillary Clinton approved a campaign plan to "stir up a scandal" against Donald Trump by "tying him to Putin." The referral was sent to FBI Director Comey, and the documents—authenticated as declassified government materials—explicitly state this allegation. However, while the claim asserts the plan aimed to link Trump to "the Russians’ hacking of the Democratic National Committee," the evidence indicates the CIA referral itself references "Russian hackers hampering U.S. elections" broadly, without specifically naming the DNC hack in that particular memo. Other declassified documents (e.g., Brennan’s notes) do include the DNC reference, but the core evidence from the CIA referral does not explicitly confirm that part.

Given the strong alignment with the documented allegation and the existence of authentic, declassified evidence supporting the majority of the claim’s elements, the conclusion is that the claim is substantively supported by the available documents.

`supported`

### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
Declassified U.S. government documents, officially released by the Director of National Intelligence, confirm that a [CIA referral](https://news.yahoo.com/dni-releases-cia-documents-hillary-204337457.html) was sent to FBI Director James Comey in 2016 documenting a Russian intelligence analysis alleging that Hillary Clinton approved a plan "to stir up a scandal" against Donald Trump by "tying him to Putin." The documents, including [former CIA Director Brennan's handwritten notes](https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/09-29-20_Letter%20to%20Sen.%20Graham_Declassification%20of%20FBI's%20Crossfire%20Hurricane%20Investigations_20-00912_U_SIGNED-FINAL.pdf), substantiate the core elements of the claim. However, while [Brennan's notes](https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/09-29-20_Letter%20to%20Sen.%20Graham_Declassification%20of%20FBI's%20Crossfire%20Hurricane%20Investigations_20-00912_U_SIGNED-FINAL.pdf) reference the DNC, the CIA referral itself refers more broadly to "Russian hackers hampering U.S. elections" without specifically naming the DNC hack. Notably, the Intelligence Community explicitly stated it [did not know the accuracy of the allegation](https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/09-29-20_Letter%20to%20Sen.%20Graham_Declassification%20of%20FBI's%20Crossfire%20Hurricane%20Investigations_20-00912_U_SIGNED-FINAL.pdf) or whether the underlying Russian intelligence analysis reflected exaggeration or fabrication, and [Comey testified he did not remember receiving the referral](https://news.yahoo.com/dni-releases-cia-documents-hillary-204337457.html). The claim is assessed as **supported**, as the declassified documents substantively corroborate the described referral and its contents, though the DNC-specific linkage rests more on Brennan's notes than the CIA memo itself.